WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.160
[Vincent] I was wondering how someone who has just graduated college and is

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:12.900
attempting (to be) a successful entrepreneur. I was just wondering what outlet you would recommend they learn business through.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.950
[Kevin] Oh, look who's arrived!

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.670
Its Savannah, my daughter. What if she has a question?

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.880
All right, Vincent the first a quick music interlude here. I was thinking sort of a...

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.460
That's just a little ditty kind of a bluesy ditty

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.540
to sort of cover up the sound of my daughter making all that noise in the background here, but let's listen to

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.239
Vincent's question. Vincent what do you got?

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:05.610
[Vincent] Hey, Mr. Wonderful, this is Vincent Ruggieri again from Vinny Theory on YouTube. This is my second question

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:12.269
so I might just scratch the first one, but I was wondering how someone who has just graduated college and is

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.199
attempting to be an Entrepreneur.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:24.659
A successful Entrepreneur could go about launching a product or starting a business in a way where they can learn business

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:32.820
through books or I was just wondering what outlet you would recommend they learn business through in a way

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:40.169
that's actually applicable that they can execute on in a real business manner rather than go to a school that teaches you

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.440
basically nothing that you're going to use once you graduate.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.340
[Kevin] Vincent I love that skepticism about school.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.640
There's nothing wrong with school I'll tell you what's great about school, business schools even, or any university or even high school

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.120
people you meet. You're gonna forget what they teach you but you're going to have those relationships the rest of your life.

00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:01.919
Now to your question, I wrote a book called "Men, Women &amp; Money". I think it's worth reading.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.149
It's a short read but it gives you a lot of basic principles about how you have to

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.790
deal with life and having said that check that out on Amazon.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.460
I think it's wonderful if I published three of them, but try "Men, Women &amp; Money", the first one.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.060
Now

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.540
Here's what I've always felt is important about entrepreneurship

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.060
go work for somebody else for a while.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.060
Try out some business that you think you'd be good at and apply yourself to that.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:32.140
Don't necessarily start up on your own for the first time. That's a tremendous amount of work and it doesn't always work out.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.820
Experience is the one thing you can never have

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.840
unless you spend time getting it, you know what I mean?

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:44.040
And it's actually the most valuable thing and it's something you should respect. A lot of people do not respect experience.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:50.849
I do. I always ask mentors and people that have done things before me what I can learn from?

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.399
Now I'm not against going to college nothing wrong with that

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:58.020
but the experience of life of actually doing something is the most powerful way to learn.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.640
So get out there and do it again, and I'm not ignoring your first question, that was a good one too.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.839
I like all the questions, we all learn together. That's what "Ask Mr. Wonderful" is all about.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:13.100
But thanks for that one Vincent. Music Interlude? Maybe, maybe, maybe. What do I got? What do I got? I'm thinking...

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.240
Yeah, maybe a little Blues.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:23.460
Oh, yeah.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:30.580
Woo.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.320
Yeah.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.540
Classic. Those three chords, D A E.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.380
Alright Sean, What do you got? Let's see.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.340
[Sean] Hey, Mr. Wonderful. Two questions...

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:50.499
What are your thoughts on using technical analysis when trading stocks and

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:56.200
can one realistically expect to become wealthy off of trading alone. Thanks a bunch!

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.260
[Kevin] Very interesting, Sean.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.300
Now, you know

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:06.000
there's two schools of thought of a technical analysis that you can learn from what happened in the past to a stock price

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.940
by applying technical analysis to try and determine what's going to do in the future.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.580
I have not found that to be very effective.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:17.640
It's not that I'm against it. I do use charts a lot which are very inexpensive

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.660
You can get them for free on any stock...

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:27.219
platform just to see what the history has been, what the pricing has been and obviously if a stock is very volatile.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:27.880
It makes me nervous.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.900
In other words, there's a huge change in fluctuation of price, but that's what traders like.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.480
Now about trading to make a living trading

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.080
really, really, really, hard.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:45.660
That's a fact. It's extremely difficult to make money trading. It's a very nimble skill. I'm not saying you can't do it.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.929
I prefer to be an investor. I own stocks for the long term.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:55.540
I use ETF's baskets of stocks and even do some fixed income with ETFs on money market things

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.969
so I can get 2% of my cash, but...

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.029
you know if you want to make a living as a

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:07.239
as a trader, to me it's akin to being a gambler in Las Vegas. Very few make money

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:12.670
and I'm not saying you can't try. I'm not against it, but for me, it's not the way I want to work.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.000
I would rather

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.040
create wealth as an entrepreneur in a business and then take some of my proceeds and invested it in the market.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:24.640
Gives me diversification in the long run that's the only free lunch in investing, but I'm definitely not a trader.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.880
But if you are good luck.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.320
You know, try it for a couple of years. If you can actually make a living doing that

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:34.390
maybe you can go longer but I doubt it. I'm a skeptic. Sorry about that.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.460
Alright, Sean.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:43.960
Okay, so here we on the set of Shark Tank and what matters is, what watch are you wearing?

